  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "KRouter",
      "text": "**Your coding agents remember what you decided — and what you changed your mind about.**\n\nClaude Code, Codex, Cursor and DeepSeek Harness share one memory: a folder of plain Markdown in [Obsidian](https://obsidian.md) that you can read and edit. Ask \"how did we decide to ship?\" and the agent answers with the page it came from. A correction replaces the old answer instead of competing with it. When the vault does not know, the agent is told so, rather than handed something close."
    },
    {
      "section": "KRouter",
      "text": "[](https://github.com/398894496-arch/KRouter/actions/workflows/ci.yml) [](https://github.com/398894496-arch/KRouter/releases/latest) [](LICENSE) · 中文：[`README.zh.md`](README.zh.md)"
    },
    {
      "section": "KRouter",
      "text": "**Measured, not claimed.** On 200 held-out questions in English and Chinese that it was never tuned on, at default\nsettings, KRouter cites a wrong page 26% of the time; claude-mem 64%, basic-memory 78%. Of 80 questions the vault\ncannot answer, KRouter refuses 47; the other two refuse none. Of 60 questions about a decision that was later\nchanged, it gets 50 right; they get 34 and 16. Neither of them can refuse or knows a page was replaced, so part of\nthat gap is by construction: plain full-text search given KRouter's two rules is level with it. What KRouter adds is\nhaving those rules on by default, plus receipts and a vault you can edit. [How it was measured](docs/BENCHMARK.md#third-bench-200-held-out-questions-on-a-vault-nobody-tunes-on-2026-10-13) · [rerun it in one second](#check-it-yourself)"
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "```bash\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/398894496-arch/KRouter/main/scripts/bootstrap.sh | sh\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "Windows (PowerShell): `irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/398894496-arch/KRouter/main/scripts/bootstrap.ps1 | iex` · Claude Code plugin: `/plugin install krouter --marketplace 398894496-arch/KRouter` (Claude Code 2.1.275 or later; from a shell, `claude plugin install krouter --marketplace 398894496-arch/KRouter` needs 2.1.292). Third-party marketplaces do not auto-update: run `claude plugin update krouter@krouter`, or turn it on in `/plugin` → Marketplaces → krouter → Enable auto-update."
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "Rather look first? Nothing below changes your machine until the last line:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/398894496-arch/KRouter.git && cd KRouter\n./scripts/first_run.sh                      # a receipt on the bundled sample vault\npython3 scripts/bootstrap.py --dry-run      # every change the install would make, made nowhere\npython3 scripts/bootstrap.py --vault ~/notes --no-timer   # install against your vault, nightly job off"
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "Python 3 is the only requirement. The installer makes a vault (or uses yours with `--vault`, overwriting nothing), connects Claude Code and Codex to it through MCP, and stops there: lookup only, no questions, nothing leaves the machine. When you want it to start remembering, run the line it prints last (`bootstrap.py --ask`), which asks two questions."
    },
    {
      "section": "Five minutes later",
      "text": "1. Ask your agent: *\"what did we decide about X last week?\"* It answers from the vault, names the page, and says plainly when the vault has nothing on it.\n2. **\"Import the agent sessions already on this machine?\"** (with `--ask`) On a yes, the last 7 days of Claude Code, Codex and Cursor agent sessions become pages, one per session: your questions and each turn's closing answer. Key-shaped text is redacted first, and nothing leaves the machine.\n3. **\"Have your model pull out what was decided?\"** On a yes, the model you already use (a logged-in CLI or an API key) writes \"what was decided, what changed\" at the top of each recent session's page. The installer tells you how many characters that sends before it sends anything."
    },
    {
      "section": "Five minutes later",
      "text": "From then on a nightly job, which also asks first, imports yesterday, distills it, and keeps the vault tidy. Nothing needs maintaining."
    },
    {
      "section": "Is it for you?",
      "text": "**Yes**, if you use Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or DeepSeek Harness (often more than one), keep notes in Markdown or\nwould, and keep re-explaining the same decision to your agents. **No**, if you want semantic search over\nembeddings, the last chat replayed into the next one, a hosted memory API, or a brain that is full on install.\n\n**Not only for code:** the same story for short-video work, recorded the same way (Claude Code with the plugin, a\ndemo vault, real output):"
    },
    {
      "section": "Is it for you?",
      "text": "**Not needed:** Obsidian (it is an optional viewer), an alias table to maintain, an environment variable to set.\nAlready have a vault? `--vault` changes none of its files. The default is lookup only: no nightly job, no session\nimport."
    },
    {
      "section": "How it is different",
      "text": "| | KRouter | Typical agent memory |\n|---|---|---|\n| Where memory lives | Markdown pages you can open, edit, diff and sync | A database or vector store |\n| After a correction | The new page replaces the old one, which is marked superseded | Old and new chunks both match; you hope the right one wins |\n| When nothing matches | `recall: none` — the agent says the vault does not know | The nearest chunk, presented with confidence |\n| Every answer | Carries its source page and a receipt | Usually unsourced |\n| Who shares it | One vault for every agent on the machine (MCP, skill, rules) | Usually one tool |"
    },
    {
      "section": "How it is different",
      "text": "**Coming from [claude-mem](https://github.com/thedotmack/claude-mem)?** Keep it. The importer has been run end to end against a live claude-mem 13.35.0 install. The installer finds its memory and offers to import it: session summaries become pages, and each project's decisions become one page you confirm. Your model can then sort those decisions into the ones still in force and the ones a later decision replaced, which claude-mem itself does not do. The Claude Code plugin also does what makes claude-mem feel like memory, in plain Markdown: when a session ends it is recorded, and when a new one starts, this repository's confirmed decisions and recent corrections come with it, each naming its page. Repositories are told apart by their path, never by folder name. Both happen only after you said yes to importing."
    },
    {
      "section": "What leaves your machine",
      "text": "Queries never do: lookup is a local SQLite full-text index, about 110 ms. Session import is local. Text goes to a model only for the steps you said yes to: distilling sessions and nightly upkeep. It goes to the CLI or API key you already use, and `--dry-run` shows the size first. Keys and pages marked secret are never sent or quoted. For the nightly writer, the API-key lane runs nothing on\nyour machine; CLI lanes differ in what they can read, and the install says which one you are choosing. Details:\n[`SECURITY.md`](SECURITY.md)."
    },
    {
      "section": "What leaves your machine",
      "text": "**Backups.** `backup.py init --dir <a folder that syncs elsewhere>` turns on a nightly encrypted archive of the whole\nvault, history included, opened again each night before it counts. Point it at iCloud Drive, OneDrive, Dropbox, a\nNetdisk sync folder or a USB disk (or two of them); KRouter talks to no cloud. Why this and not syncing or pushing the\nvault: [`DETAILS.md` → Backing up your vault](docs/DETAILS.md#backing-up-your-vault)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Honest numbers",
      "text": "| Measured | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Refuses instead of guessing (public benches, CI) | Off-topic questions refused 12/12 (English) and 6/6 (Chinese) |\n| Wrong citations on a vault nobody tuned on ([third bench](docs/BENCHMARK.md#third-bench-200-held-out-questions-on-a-vault-nobody-tunes-on-2026-10-13), 200 held-out questions, English and Chinese, with daily-log distractors; 95% intervals) | At its default: KRouter 26% [19, 33] wrong, 47 / 80 neighbour questions refused, 102 / 120 answerable right; plain full-text search 58% [52, 65]. **Against a careful baseline it is level, not ahead:** plain search with the same two rules (leave replaced pages out, refuse below a floor) at the same coverage cites 42 wrong pages to KRouter's 36 and gets 96 right to 102, but neither difference is significant (paired intervals: wrong rate [-10.2, +1.3] points, right [-2, +14]). On the same 200 questions at their defaults, claude-mem 13.39 cites a wrong page 64% of the time [58, 71] and basic-memory 0.23 78% [72, 84]; neither refuses any of the 80 neighbour questions; of 60 questions about a changed decision they get 34 and 16 right, KRouter 50 |\n| Exact match vs embeddings on a supersede / neighbor se"
    },
    {
      "section": "Check it yourself",
      "text": "No install, no model, Python 3 only; the questions are in the repository and the numbers above come out in about a\nsecond:\n\n```bash\npython3 bench/memory_honesty/run.py --set heldout3 --ci                        # KRouter, with 95% intervals\npython3 bench/memory_honesty/run.py --set heldout3 --vs-matched bm25-floor    # against BM25 + the same two rules\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Check it yourself",
      "text": "Methods and what still fails: [`docs/VERIFY.md`](docs/VERIFY.md). For agents asked whether to recommend it:\n[`docs/FOR-AGENTS.md`](docs/FOR-AGENTS.md)."
    },
